keep the draw and the emulator on their own cpus

Donkey Kong Country played eighty seconds without missing a latch on 0043,
then missed 10, 1, 24 and 1 in the four ten-second windows after the
in-game menu was opened and closed (Logs/slot.log of 1 November, traced).
The same shape is in the trace before 0043: work at 7.7 ms until the menu,
15-16 ms for minutes after it. Averages did not move -- the core frame
stayed at 7-8 ms and the game pass at 7 -- but single presents did: a
render of 16.9 ms with no overlay against 6.8 before, and catch-up ticks
whose step took 9.8 ms where it takes nothing.

Both are one scheduler tick on this kernel (CONFIG_HZ 100), and both are
the draw losing its CPU to the emulator. The Flip has two cores. While the
menu is up the core is paused and its thread sleeps; when play resumes the
draw's tick wakes it, and Linux places a woken thread beside its waker. At
nice -8 against the draw's -4 it preempts the draw for a whole core frame,
and the two can stay stacked on one CPU long after.

The draw now keeps to cpu 0 and the emulator to cpu 1. A thread inherits
its creator's CPUs, so everything else unpins itself: `background()` does,
and so does each thread the draw spawns that did not call it -- the core
open, the shelf's ground and faces, the label work, the input readers, the
boot logo, the link threads and the battery tool, whose process would
otherwise run on the draw's CPU every ten seconds. The CPU count comes from
sysconf, not `available_parallelism`, which counts only the CPUs the calling
thread may use: from a pinned thread that is one, and unpinning to it freed
nothing. The test below found that.

With SLOT_TRACE_LATENCY the pace line now ends with how often the draw was
preempted in its window, from the thread's nonvoluntary context switches,
so the next trace says whether this was the cause rather than leaving it
to be inferred again.

Tests, native and as armv7 under qemu: a pinned thread keeps to its cpu, a
thread it spawns inherits that, and one that goes to the background is
free of it again; the preemption count reads. slot and slot-power's lib
tests pass; slot's integration tests need the host feature and were not
run.

Applies on top of 0044, upstream 91499a9.

